     1	\section{Multi-round variations of SET}\label{sec:multiround}
     2	
     3	In this and the next section we talk about multi-round variations of SET where
     4	the number of values (or in other words the size of the Sets) is 3. 
     5	In this case, each card (vertex of the hypergraph) is described by a 
     6	vector in $\mathbb{F}_3^{n}$. Note that, three cards form a Set if and only 
     7	if their corresponding vectors add up to the all-$0$ vector. It is also easy to
     8	observe that every pair of cards can have up to one card that forms a Set with
     9	the other two. This property will prove useful later.
    10	
    11	We will once again use a hypergraph formulation, though
    12	different from the one in the previous section. Specifically, we consider the
    13	$3$-uniform hypergraph formed if we construct a vertex for each dealt card and
    14	a hyperedge (that is, a set of size 3) for each Set. It is clear that given a
    15	SET instance, one can in polynomial time construct this hypergraph. 
    16	
    17	We will first talk about a maximization variation: given a set of cards we ask the question
    18	whether there exist at least $r$ Sets that we can pick up before leaving no
    19	Sets on the table. We call this problem \maxset. Observe that this problem is a
    20	special case of 3-\pack, which is a known NP-hard problem. We thus need to show
    21	that the problem remains NP-hard when restricted to instances realizable by SET
    22	cards. This is established in Theorem \ref{thm:maxset}.
    23	
    24	Then, we turn our attention to a minimization version: given a set of cards, is
    25	it possible by removing at most $r$ Sets ($3r$ cards) to eliminate all potential Sets?
    26	We call this problem \minset. This problem is a special case of \eds\ in 3-uniform hypergraphs. We show its
    27	NP-hardness even when restricted to hypergraphs realizable by SET cards. Then, we prove that 
    28	the natural parameterized version of \eds\ in 3-uniform hypergraphs with parameter $r$ is FPT, 
    29	thus proving that the special case of a parameterization of this version of SET
    30	is also FPT.
    31	
    32	\subsection{NP-Hardness the maximization version}\label{sec:maxset}
    33	
    34	\begin{theorem} \label{thm:maxset} 
    35	\maxset\ is NP-Hard.  
    36	\end{theorem}
    37	
    38	\begin{proof}
    39	
    40	We design a reduction from 3-SAT. Given a formula $\phi$ of 3-SAT we first
    41	create an equivalent formula $\phi$' where each clause contains at most 3
    42	literals and each variable appears exactly 3 times (two as positive and one as
    43	negative or two as negative and one as positive). Furthermore, any two clauses
    44	of $\phi$' share at most one variable. A similar construction appears in
    45	\cite{papad}, but it is also presented below for the sake of completeness. 
    46	
    47	\begin{lemma} \label{specialSAT}
    48	Any formula $\phi$ of regular \emph{3-SAT} can be transformed into an equivalent formula $\phi$', where each clause has at most 3 variables and each variable appears exactly 3 times in $\phi$' (not all positive or all negative).
    49	\end{lemma}
    50	
    51	\begin{proof}
    52	Given a formula $\phi$ of regular 3-SAT, we create an equivalent formula $\phi$' as follows: first, we ensure that each variable appears at least 4 times (if not, we double some of the clauses where this variable appears); then, for each appearance of each variable $v$ we create a new variable $v_i$ for $i=1, \ldots l$, where $l$ is the total number of appearances, and clauses $(\neg v_i \vee v_{i+1})$, $(\neg v_l \vee v_1)$.
    53	
    54	Clearly all variables in $\phi$' appear exactly 3 times and not all positive or all negative. Furthermore, $\phi$ is satisfiable iff $\phi$' is satisfiable by an assignment that sets the same truth value to all variables $v_i$ in $\phi$' corresponding to the same variable $v$ in $\phi$. \qed
    55	
    56	\end{proof}
    57	
    58	Let $m$ be the number of clauses of $\phi$' and $n$ the number of
    59	variables.
    60	
    61	The main idea of the reduction is as follows: from formula $\phi$' we create an
    62	instance of \maxset\ which consists of variable gadgets (one corresponding to
    63	each variable) and clause gadgets (one corresponding to each clause). The
    64	variable gadget of a variable $x$ contains five cards: three cards $x_{1}$,
    65	$x_{2}$ and $x_{3}$ for each appearance of $x$ in $\phi$' ($x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$
    66	corresponding to appearances with the same sign and $x_3$ to opposite), and two
    67	more cards: $x_{12}$ which forms a Set with $x_1$ and $x_2$, and $x_{123}$
    68	which forms a Set with $x_{3}$ and $x_{12}$. Picking either Set is equivalent
    69	to making an assignment to $x$ (both Sets contain $x_{12}$, only one Set can be
    70	formed leaving either positive or negative appearances of $x$ unused). The
    71	cards $x_1,x_2,x_3$ will also appear in the clause gadgets and, intuitively, we
    72	will be able to select a Set from a clause gadget if and only if one of its
    73	$x_i$ vertices is free, corresponding to a true literal.
    74	
    75	\begin{figure}[ht]
    76	\begin{minipage}[b]{0.45\linewidth}
    77	\centering
    78	\includegraphics[scale=0.8]{variables}
    79	\caption{The variable gadget}
    80	\label{fig:variables}
    81	\end{minipage}
    82	\hspace{0.5cm}
    83	\begin{minipage}[b]{0.45\linewidth}
    84	\centering
    85	\includegraphics[scale=0.8]{clauses}
    86	\caption{The clause gadget}
    87	\label{fig:clauses}
    88	\end{minipage}
    89	\end{figure}
    90	
    91	
    92	The clause gadget consists of four additional cards: one card per literal in
    93	the clause $c_1$, $c_2$, and $c_3$, and one additional card $c_m$ (for clauses
    94	of size 2 we do not introduce $c_3$).  Furthermore, each card $x_{c_i}$
    95	corresponding to the literal in the $i^{th}$ position of a clause $c$ forms a
    96	Set with cards $c_i$ and $c_m$. In order to be able to pick this Set (and
    97	satisfy $c$) $x_{c_i}$ should not have been picked during the assignment phase. 
    98	
    99	%Now let's present the reduction in more detail. 
   100	
   101	Observe that, if one sees the
   102	new instance as a 3-\pack\ instance, it is not hard to establish that the
   103	instance has a solution of size $n+m$ if and only if $\phi$' is satisfiable.
   104	The bonus point is that this instance is realizable with Set cards. In 
   105	what follows we focus our attention to proving this fact. \qed
   106	
   107	Each card will be described by a vector in $\mathbb{F}_3^{m+n+1}$. 
   108	The first $n+1$ coordinates constitute
   109	the variable part and the last $m$ the clause part. The variable part is the
   110	same for all cards in each variable gadget representing variable $i$: it
   111	consists of all $0$s, except for the $i^{th}$ coordinate which is set to $1$.
   112	Similarly, vectors of clause gadgets have the same clause part: again all $0$s,
   113	except the ${(n+1+j)}^{th}$ coordinate is set to $1$ for the $j^{th}$ clause.  We
   114	have now fully specified the vectors for the $x_i$'s. Let us explain how the
   115	remaining vectors are filled out.
   116	
   117	\begin{itemize}
   118	
   119	\item $x_{12}$: clause part is equal to the clause part of $-x_1 - x_2$, so
   120	that $x_1+x_2+x_{12} = 0^m \bmod{3}$;
   121	%and thus it consists of 0s except of two 2s in positions $i$,
   122	%$j$, which denotes that variable $x$ appears with the same sign in clauses $i$
   123	%and $j$.
   124	
   125	\item $x_{123}$: clause part is equal to clause part of $- x_3 - x_{12}$; 
   126	%Thus, it contains 0s
   127	%in all but three positions; in positions $i$, $j$ as above the value is 1, and
   128	%in position $k$ (with $k^{th}$ clause being the one where variable $x$ appears
   129	%with opposite sign), where the value is 2.  
   130	
   131	\item $c_{m}$: variable part is equal to variable part of $x_{c_1} + x_{c_2} +
   132	x_{c_3}$, if they exist.  If clause has only two literals, we only use
   133	$x_{c_1}+x_{c_2}$ for the first $n$ coordinates while coordinate $n+1$ is set
   134	to 1. The intuition behind introducing the dummy 1 at position $n+1$ for
   135	clauses of size 2 is that it will be convenient if we always know that the
   136	variable part of $c_m$ has three $1$'s.
   137	
   138	\item $c_{1}$: variable part is equal to  variable part of $x_{c_1} - x_{c_2} -
   139	x_{c_3}$ ($c_{2}$, $c_{3}$ are formed accordingly). Again, if $x_{c_3}$ does
   140	not exist we use $x_{c_1} - x_{c_2}$ and set coordinate $n+1$ to 2. 
   141	
   142	\end{itemize} 
   143	
   144	For a detailed presentation of the values of the different types of cards see
   145	table \ref{table}.
