   152	\hline
   153	$x_i$ & (0, 0, \ldots 0, 1, 0, 0 \ldots 0) & (0, 0, \ldots 0, 1, 0, 0 \ldots 0) \\
   154	$x_{12}$ & (0, 0, \ldots 0, 1, 0, 0 \ldots 0) & (0, 0, \ldots 0, 2, 0, 0 \ldots 0 2, 0 \ldots 0) \\
   155	$x_{123}$ & (0, 0, \ldots 0, 1, 0, 0 \ldots 0) & (0, 1, 0, \ldots 0, 2, 0, 0 \ldots 0 1, 0 \ldots 0) \\
   156	$c_{i}$ & (0, 1, \ldots 0, 2, 0, 0 \ldots 0 2, 0 \ldots 0) &(0, 0, \ldots 0, 1, 0, 0 \ldots 0) \\
   157	$c_m$ & (0, 1, \ldots 0, 1, 0, 0 \ldots 0 1, 0 \ldots 0) & (0, 0, \ldots 0, 1, 0, 0 \ldots 0)\\
   158	\hline
   159	\end{tabular}
   160	\caption{A synopsis of all possible tuples of the different types of card values for proof of Theorem \ref{thm:maxset}.}
   161	\label{table}
   162	\end{table}
   163	
   164	Now, we prove that the only Sets which are formed are indeed the Sets that we described
   165	in the introduction of Section \ref{sec:maxset}. To achieve this we need to prove the following 3 Lemmata:
   166	
   167	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:same} 
   168	Cards of formed Sets share either the same variable part or the same clause part.
   169	\end{lemma}
   170	
   171	\begin{proof} 
   172	
   173	First, observe that if two vectors agree in either the clause or the variable
   174	part then the third vector should also agree with them. Therefore, we will only
   175	consider Sets that contain a card of type $c_i$ or $c_m$, because in a Set
   176	containing only cards from the variable gadgets, their vectors should agree
   177	on the variable part.
   178	
   179	Suppose that there exists a Set where the 3 cards share neither their variable
   180	part nor their clause part. Since a card of type $c_i$ (or $c_m$) is part of
   181	this Set, then a card of type $c_m$ (or $c_i$ accordingly) should also be part
   182	of it (each of these two cards has three non-zero values in their variable part
   183	and there is no other way to match them with two other cards from variable
   184	gadgets which have only one non-zero value). So this Set should contain a card
   185	of type $c_i$ and a card of type $c_m$. 
   186	
   187	Since the two cards we have ($c_i$ and $c_m$) do not agree on their clause
   188	part, the third card of a Set must have exactly two coordinates set to 2 in its
   189	clause part, and all others to 0. Therefore, it must be of type $x_{12}$. The
   190	two 2s of card $x_{12}$ should be aligned with the 1s from $c_i$ and $c'_m$,
   191	when $c$ and $c'$ are different clauses.  But for variable parts to agree,
   192	non-zero values in cards $c_i$ and $c'_m$ should be aligned, which means that
   193	clauses $c$ and $c'$ should contain identical variables.  However that is not
   194	possible from the construction of $\phi$' where different clauses share no more
   195	than one common variable. \qed
   196	
   197	\end{proof}
   198	
   199	
   200	\begin{lemma} \label{lem:variable} 
   201	Only two different types of Sets are formed
   202	by cards that share the same variable part and they intersect.  
   203	\end{lemma}
   204	
   205	\begin{proof} 
   206	
   207	By construction, there are two different Sets formed within a variable gadget
   208	as shown in figure \ref{fig:variables}. Furthermore, each pair of cards $a$,
   209	$b$ has a unique third card $-(a+b) \bmod 3$ with which they form a Set. Only
   210	possible triplet where cards are pairwise not in participation of existing Sets
   211	are cards $x_1$ (or equivalently $x_2$), $x_3$, and $x_{123}$ which can't form
   212	a Set. \qed
   213	\end{proof}
   214	
   215	\begin{lemma} \label{lem:clause}
   216	Sets of cards that share the same clause part shall contain a card of type $c_m$.
   217	\end{lemma}
   218	
   219	\begin{proof} Cards of the same clause type are $x_i$, $c_i$ and $c_m$. A card of type $c_i$ can't exist alone with two cards of type $x_i$ because its variable part has three non-zero values and can't match with two cards where each of them has only one non-zero value. Trying to put two cards of type $c_i$ in the same Set won't work either: at least one pair of 2s should be aligned, which means that the last card should also have a 2 in that position. This only leaves a third card of type $c_i$ as a possibility (no other type has a 2 in the variable part). The only way three cards of this type could potential match is if all non-zero values are matched, which would produce three identical cards. \qed
   220	\end{proof}
   221	
   222	Observe now that if $\phi$' is satisfiable, then we can select one Set from
   223	each variable gadget (using the corresponding variable's assignment) and one
   224	Set from each clause gadget (since one of the literals is set to True). This
   225	gives $n+m$ Sets. For the converse direction, observe that, from Lemmata
   226	\ref{lem:variable} and \ref{lem:clause} it is not possible to select more than
   227	one Set from each gadget. Thus, one can extract a satisfying assignment for
   228	$\phi$' from a solution of size $n+m$. \qed
   229	
   230	\end{proof}
   231	
   232	\subsection{Results on the minimization version}
   233	
   234	Next, we present yet another multi-round version of SET, \minset. 
   235	We remind the reader that in this problem a single player is trying to remove 
   236	the smallest possible number of Sets so
   237	that no more Sets are left on the table. Each card, as before, has an unbounded number of
   238	attributes and each attribute can take 3 values.
   239	
   240	We prove that \minset\ is NP-hard via a simple reduction from \eds\ (proven NP-hard in \cite{garey}).
   241	
   242	\begin{theorem} \label{thm:minset}
   243	
   244	\minset\ is NP-hard.
   245	
   246	\end{theorem}
   247	
   248	\begin{proof}
   249	
   250	Given an instance of \eds\ (a graph $G(V,E)$ and a number $r$), we create an
   251	instance of \minset\ of $|V|+|E|$ cards with $|V|$ dimensions each, such that
   252	if $G$ has an edge dominating set of size at most $r$ then there exist at most
   253	$r$ Sets which once picked up destroy all other Sets. Again, cards will be
   254	represented by vectors in $\mathbb{F}_3^{|V|}$.
   255	
   256	The construction is as follows: For each vertex $i\in V$ we create a card where
   257	all coordinates are 0 except from the value of the $i^{th}$ coordinate which is
   258	equal to 1.  Furthermore, for each edge $(i,j) \in E$ we create a card where
   259	all coordinates are 0 except from the values of coordinates $i$ and $j$ which
   260	are equal to 2. 
   261	
   262	Observe that the only Sets formed correspond directly to edges in $G$. Picking
   263	a Set corresponding to edge $(i,j)$ eliminates the cards corresponding to
   264	vertices $i$, $j$ (together with the card corresponding to edge $(i,j)$). This
   265	move causes the elimination of any potential Set containing cards corresponding
   266	to vertices $i$ and $j$. Thus an edge dominating set of size at most $r$ in $G$
   267	corresponds to an equal number of Sets overlapping all other Sets. On the other
   268	hand the smallest number of Sets that overlap all other Sets is equal to the
   269	minimum edge dominating set. \qed
   270	
   271	\end{proof}
   272	
   273	Since the \minset\ problem is hard, it makes sense to consider its naturally
   274	parameterized version: Given an arbitrary set of cards, do there exist $r$ Sets
   275	that overlap all other formed Sets? We show that a simple FTP algorithm
   276	can decide this question. As a matter of fact, the algorithm works on any 3-uniform 
   277	hypergraph. Recall that the similar
   278	parameterization of the maximization problem is also known
   279	to be FPT, by relevant results on 3-\pack\ \cite{ChenFLLW11}.  
   280	
   281	\begin{theorem} \label{thm:edsfpt}
   282	\eds\ in 3-uniform hypergraphs parameterized by the size of the edge dominating 
   283	set is FPT.
   284	\end{theorem}
   285	
   286	\begin{proof}
   287	
   288	We give an algorithm that follows the same basic ideas as the FPT algorithm for
   289	\eds\ given in \cite{Fernau06}. We will not worry too much about optimizing the
   290	parameter dependence, instead focusing on establishing fixed-parameter
   291	tractability.
   292	
   293	Consider the 3-uniform hypergraph formed as follows: we have a vertex for every
   294	given card and a hyperedge of size 3 for each Set of the input instance.
   295	Suppose that there exists a set of $r$ Sets such that removing the cards they
   296	consist of would destroys all Sets. Then, there must exist a hitting set in this
   297	hypergraph of size exactly $3r$ (since the $r$ removed Sets cannot overlap).
   298	
   299	We will list all hitting sets of size $3r$ with a simple branching algorithm as
   300	follows: start with an empty hitting set and as long as the size of the
   301	currently selected hitting set has size $<3r$ find a hyperedge that is
   302	currently not covered. For each non-empty subset of the vertices of this
   303	hyperedge (there are 7 choices) add these vertices to the hitting set and
   304	remove all hyperedges they hit. Recursively continue until either all
   305	hyperedges are hit or the hitting set has size more than $3r$. If we have a
   306	hitting set of size exactly $3r$ add it to the list.
   307	
   308	For each hitting set $S$ of size exactly $3r$ do the following: check if the
   309	hypergraph induced by $S$ has a perfect matching, that is, a set of $r$
   310	disjoint hyperedges covering all vertices. This can be done in time exponential
   311	in $r$. If the answer is yes, we have found a set of $r$ Sets that overlaps all
   312	other Sets. If the answer is no for all hitting sets then we can reject. \qed
   313	
   314	\end{proof}
   315	
   316	\begin{corollary} \label{cor:setfpt}
   317	\minset\ parameterized by the number of Sets that will be picked is FPT.
   318	\end{corollary}
   319	
   320	Corollary \ref{cor:setfpt} follows directly from Theorem \ref{thm:edsfpt}.
     1	\section{Conclusions and Open Problems} \label{sec:conclusions}
     2	
     3	In this paper we studied the computational complexity of the game of SET and
     4	presented some interesting connections with other well-studied problems, such
     5	as \rdm, \eds\ and \pack. 
     6	
     7	The one-round case of SET is now fairly well-understood. However there are
     8	quite a few interesting open problems one might consider in the multi-round
     9	case, especially the two-player version \tpset.  It remains unknown whether this game is
    10	PSPACE-Complete. However, proving the hardness of \arc\ on graphs would settle the complexity
    11	of this problem as well (which is an interesting open question on its own
    12	accord). Staying on \arc, it might be interesting to show whether the game played
    13	on general 3-uniform hypergraphs is FPT. We remind the reader that our proof that 
    14	\tpset\ is FPT is based on the property of SET that each pair of cards can have at 
    15	most one third with which they all form a Set. That property is vital 
    16	for the proof since it establishes that the line graph has essentially bounded degree. 
    17	This is not true for a general 3-uniform hypergraph though.

